        revenueText: "Projects and commercial ACs were **55% of FY26 revenue** (₹6,763cr), room ACs and refrigeration **43%** (₹5,332cr) and professional electronics **2%**. Data-centre contracting brought about ₹1,000cr of revenue in FY26; management guides ₹1,350–1,400cr in FY27 and about ₹4,000cr (about 20% of the company) by FY29. The order book was ₹7,764cr in June 2026.",
        mixes: [
            { caption: 'Revenue by segment, FY26 (Q4 FY26 call)', parts: [{ label: 'Projects and commercial ACs', share: 55 }, { label: 'Room ACs and refrigeration', share: 43 }, { label: 'Professional electronics', share: 2 }] },
        ],
        customers: ["**Builders, factories and data-centre owners**, who buy contracting and commercial ACs; data-centre clients are ‘blocking our capacity’ and asking for three-year contracts.", "**Households**, buying room ACs through dealers, large stores and online, backed by 2,100+ service centres and 4,000+ channel partners.", "**Food and retail businesses** buying deep freezers and bottle coolers through 8,800+ stores, and **overseas brands** that buy its heat pumps."],
        difficulty: { level: 'High', text: "Large chillers and data-centre cooling need engineering, testing and a track record that few Indian firms have; multinationals still dominate data-centre chillers. Room ACs are much easier: about 60 brands sell them, many made by contract makers." },
        moat: "Leadership in commercial ACs, a projects track record and a large service network give it a strong position in business cooling. In room ACs its price premium did not survive Q1 FY27, when rivals with cheap old stock undercut it.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Data centres**: the ~₹3,500cr data-centre contracting market is expected to more than double in three years, and FY27 data-centre orders are guided at about ₹3,000cr; its contracts carry metal-price escalation clauses and good payment terms.", "**The summer and room-AC pricing**: industry AC volume grew 21% in Q1 FY27 and Blue Star's 18%; it held back price rises and now says ‘we should not allow market share to deteriorate’.", "**New buildings**: commercial ACs (a ~₹5,500cr market, up 9% in FY26) follow construction; projects growth could rise to 12% for a couple of years.", "**Exports**: an extra $100 million a year is targeted from FY28, depending on US tariffs.", "**Capacity and costs**: capex of ₹300–350cr a year; a decision on another 3-lakh-unit AC line at Sri City is due in October 2026, and a cheaper re-engineered entry-level AC range arrives by Q4 FY27."],
        inputs: "**Compressors** (all bought in; it does not make them), **copper** and **aluminium** for coils, **steel** sheet, **plastics** and **electronics**; about 40% of its room-AC parts are imported, so the rupee matters. Projects buy chillers and other equipment. Materials were 49% of sales in FY26; Screener puts another 29% under power and factory costs, which for a contractor largely reflects site and subcontracted work.",
        costEffect: "**Partly passed on.** In projects, data-centre contracts carry metal-price escalation clauses. In room ACs it needed a 13% price rise (5% for energy labels, 8% for commodities and the rupee) but passed on only about 5%; the MD says ‘you have to reduce costs’. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 5 points (8% to 3%). **What happened:** India's copper price index rose from 100 in Q1 FY24 to 149 in Q1 FY27, while the margin went from 6.5% to 5.2%. It held at 6.5–8.3% through FY24–FY26 as prices kept pace, then fell in Q1 FY27 when rivals would not raise prices and it chose share over margin.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'Voltas', note: 'The room-AC leader, about 3 points ahead in share and more localised.' }, { name: 'Daikin, LG, Carrier, Johnson Controls', note: 'Multinationals strong in chillers and VRF systems; they dominate data-centre chillers.' }, { name: 'Contract-made AC brands', note: 'Rivals with cheap old stock and outsourced ACs that, in Blue Star’s words, ‘operate with 3.5% to 4% margin’.' }],
        position: "**India's leader in ducted ACs (53%) and scroll chillers (48%), with about 30% of data-centre contracting**, and a leading room-AC brand with about 14% of the market by value.",
        money: "Screener shows **₹49cr of net cash** at March 2026 (borrowings ₹810cr); the company put its net cash at ₹175.5cr in March and ₹900cr in June, after the summer stock was sold. FY26 operating cash was only ₹154cr as stock rose to 131 days. Capex is ₹300–350cr in FY27 from its own cash; fixed assets doubled to ₹1,697cr in three years, and return on capital slipped from 26% to 21%. CARE rates it AA+/Stable. Promoters hold **36.49%**, unchanged.",
        watch: ["Room-AC margin in Q3.", "Data-centre orders against the ₹3,000cr FY27 target.", "The October decision on a new Sri City line."],
        charts: consumerDurablesCharts['Blue Star'],
