        customers: ["**Households**, buying mainly through multi-brand outlets, where it ranks No.2 by volume in refrigerators and washers combined.", "**Large chains and online stores**, a growing share of sales.", "**Its parent**, Whirlpool Corporation, which buys about ₹237cr a year of services from the Indian company."],
        difficulty: { level: 'Medium', text: "Refrigerators and washers need large plants, compressors and steel-forming lines, and new energy labels every three years. But several global and Indian rivals do this at scale in India, and ACs are easy to outsource." },
        moat: "A known brand, India-designed features and strong terms with dealers (working capital is usually negative). The weakness: no pricing power; a 2022 price rise it took alone caused ‘a sharp decline in market share’.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Ownership and upgrades**: refrigerators are in only the mid-30s% of Indian homes, and buyers are moving to frost-free and bigger models; industry volume grew only 4–5% in Q1 FY27.", "**The summer**: April–June is the peak for refrigerators and ACs; July–September is the weakest quarter, which management calls ‘a difficult quarter financially’.", "**Share gains in weak spots**: it wants double-digit shares in front-load washers and ACs in 2–3 years; front-load volume rose 80% and AC revenue 50% in Q1 FY27.", "**Following rivals on price**: April and May price rises helped Q1, but it raises prices only when its main rivals do.", "**Capacity is not a limit**: plants run at 70–75%, base capex is ‘about 100 plus’ crore a year, and a plan for the cash, including possible acquisitions, is due within 12 months."],
        inputs: "**Steel** sheet for cabinets and drums, **plastics** and foam (oil-linked: crude went from $67 to $97 a barrel in a year), **compressors**, **copper** and electronics, plus finished ACs from contract makers. Under 30% of inputs are imported, but more are priced in dollars, and the rupee fell from about 88 to 95–96. It also pays about 1.2% of sales to its parent as royalty and technical fees. Materials were 67% of sales in FY26.",
        costEffect: "**Not protected.** Whirlpool follows the market on price and cannot raise prices unless its key rivals do, so every cost shock hits profit until the whole industry moves. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 7 points (6% to about −1%, a loss). **What happened:** India's copper price index rose from 100 in Q1 FY24 to 149 in Q1 FY27, and the margin went from 6.0% to 5.1%. The swings were seasonal (8–9% in the summer quarters of FY25 and FY26, 3.5% in Q2 FY26), but the Q1 FY27 summer peak was only 5.1% as oil-linked plastics, the weak rupee and energy-label costs outran its price rises.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'LG and Samsung', note: 'The leaders in refrigerators and washing machines, with larger scale and more pricing power.' }, { name: 'Haier and Godrej', note: 'Strong rivals in refrigerators, including direct-cool models.' }, { name: 'IFB and Bosch', note: 'Rivals in front-load washers, where Whirlpool has only about 5%.' }, { name: 'Voltas, Blue Star and many AC brands', note: 'Whirlpool is a small AC player.' }],
        position: "**No.2 by volume in refrigerators and washing machines combined through multi-brand outlets**, No.1 in direct-cool refrigerators for several months, and a small player in front-load washers and ACs.",
        money: "Screener shows **₹2,194cr of net cash**; the company holds about ₹2,553cr of cash and bank deposits and no debt beyond leases, against a market value of {mcap:Whirlpool India|₹11,300cr}. FY26 operating cash halved to ₹298cr, and free cash was only ₹35cr (Screener) after capex on the four-door line. Dividend payout is 22% and there is no buyback. On 22 September 2026 the parent said it intends to sell its stake ‘as part of a control transaction’; a buyer would have to make an open offer. CRISIL rates it AA+/Stable. The promoter holds **39.76%**, down from 75% in September 2023.",
